{
  "acceptance": [
    "A test that records event order shows connection open and initialize() complete before the first operation interval starts and the connection closes after it ends; the test fails against the base commit.",
    "grep -n 'time.time()' benchbox/core/tpcds/maintenance_operations.py benchbox/core/tpcds/maintenance_test.py returns no line used to compute a duration.",
    "Two runs with the same configured seed against the same empty DuckDB at SF0.01 produce identical per-table row counts and per-column checksums after maintenance; a run with a different seed differs.",
    "The seed used is written to the maintenance result; default behaviour no longer reads the wall clock.",
    "Smoke: a DuckDB TPC-DS SF0.01 run with the maintenance phase completes and reports each operation's interval; paste the summary in the PR.",
    "changelog fragment states that maintenance timings from earlier versions include connection setup and initialize() and are not comparable."
  ],
  "batch": {
    "batch_id": "tpc-timer-boundaries-v2",
    "implementation_dependencies": [],
    "member_id": "tpcds-maintenance-timer-hygiene-20261103"
  },
  "context": "Origin: 2026-11-03 analysis of TPC-H RF1 timing and its adversarial reviews; the findings are restated in this item and in tpc-measured-intervals-reference-20261103. Spec clauses cited here were read from the published PDFs at the links on 2026-11-03; re-read them rather than trusting this text.\n\n2026-11-05: connection and initialize() moved out of each operation interval; monotonic durations; seeded generator (DEFAULT_MAINTENANCE_SEED, field on TPCDSMaintenanceTestConfig, recorded as result seed); DuckDB setseed through wrapped connections. Open oracle finding: production callers (platforms/base/execution.py, tpcds/benchmark/runner.py, official_benchmark.py) pass no seed from the run and drop result seed when converting to exported results. Those files are outside this item's named paths and the batch scope. The default seed is fixed so runs are reproducible; run-level seed plumbing and export is a possible follow-up item.",
  "description": "Problem (benchbox/core/tpcds/maintenance_test.py and maintenance_operations.py; re-read before editing). Each maintenance operation's timer covers opening a connection, initialize() (about 15 MIN/MAX dimension queries) and closing the connection. The inner timers use time.time(), which is a wall clock. The synthetic row generator is seeded from int(time.time()), so two runs on the same inputs insert different rows and no before/after checksum comparison is possible. These defects exist whatever data source the owner chooses in tpcds-maintenance-data-source-decision-20261103, so this item waits on no decision.\n\nTPC-DS 4.0.0 clause 7.4.9.1 starts a maintenance function's interval at the earlier of first submission or first read of the refresh data, so connection setup and initialize() are outside it.\n\nChange: open the connection and run initialize() before the interval starts and close after it ends; time with mono_time()/elapsed_seconds(); seed the generator from a configured value (default fixed) so a run is reproducible, and record the seed in the result.\n\nGuardrails for all code work: work only in a linked worktree (make worktree-create); never edit the primary clone. No explanatory comments or docstrings in source (make comment-policy-check). Durations use benchbox.utils.clock.mono_time()/elapsed_seconds(). Add a changelog.d/ fragment. Cited line numbers are orientation only: re-read the named symbols, docs and spec clauses before editing and treat a mismatch as a reason to stop and update this item. No live-cloud runs without owner approval; heavy runs only through make heavy-run or a lock-taking entry point. Green focused tests are not release certification. Do not change what the operations insert or the number of rows (the hardcoded 10 rows belongs to tpcds-maintenance-dsdgen-refresh-data-20261103 and the data-source decision). Do not add set-based SQL here (perf-tpcds-maintenance-set-based-20261031). If docs/internal/tpc-measured-intervals.md exists on the base branch, update its TPC-DS maintenance row; otherwise append the row text to the context of tpc-measured-intervals-reference-20261103.",
